# Orchestrator — per-instance configuration.
# Every value is optional; the defaults below are what you get with no env set.
# This one set of variables fully relocates an instance (fresh container, other
# user, other ports) with zero code edits. Export them in the environment that
# launches `npm run dev` / `npm start` (server.js reads them before Next boots,
# so a .env file alone is not enough for PORT/ORCH_HOSTNAME/PTY_*).

# ---- app server (server.js) ----
# Port + bind address for the single public origin (Next.js + /pty proxy).
#
# ORCH_HOSTNAME, not HOSTNAME: the generic name is injected by things that are
# not us (Fedora's /etc/profile exports the machine name to every login shell,
# Docker injects the container id), and reading it made `npm start` bind the
# wrong interface. Bare HOSTNAME is ignored entirely.
#
# The default is loopback, because a local instance is unauthenticated and hands
# out a shell — binding every interface serves that to your whole network, and
# the cross-origin gate cannot help (a LAN client just forges the Host header).
# Widen it only behind CF_ACCESS_*. The container image sets 0.0.0.0 itself.
PORT=3000
ORCH_HOSTNAME=

# How long, in milliseconds, a SIGTERM/SIGINT (docker stop / Ctrl-C) waits for
# in-flight turns to abort and persist their interrupted state — the same
# settlement a Stop-button press produces — before the process exits anyway.
# Read by both server.js and lib/config.ts; keep them the same value. Default:
# 5000 (5s), generous for the local SQLite writes involved, short enough to
# stay well under the container runtime's own SIGKILL grace period.
ORCH_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS=

# Background linger: when a Claude turn started background shell work (Bash
# run_in_background, background subagents), the session's CLI process is kept
# alive after the model stops talking — the work finishes and its completion
# notification wakes the model into a follow-up turn, instead of dying
# silently when the turn ends. While lingering the task shows "working in
# background" (with how long it's been) and new messages queue as usual.
# On by default; set to off/0/false to end turns at result time and let
# background tasks die with the CLI (the pre-feature behavior). Session
# wakeups (ScheduleWakeup / CronCreate / /loop) ride the same linger — they
# only fire while the session is held open — so with this off every pending
# wakeup is reported cancelled in the transcript at the end of the turn.
ORCH_BACKGROUND_LINGER=

# Optional deadline, in milliseconds, on a lingering turn — counted from the
# moment it first starts lingering. Default 0 = no deadline: the session
# waits until the work settles or you press Stop, and the visible "working in
# background · <age>" state is what tells you a session is worth kicking. Set
# a positive value to auto-stop lingering work at that age instead; the cut
# is recorded as a transcript notice so the next turn doesn't assume the work
# finished. A scheduled wakeup is waited for only if it falls inside this
# window; one beyond it is cancelled and named in the transcript up front.
ORCH_BACKGROUND_LINGER_MS=

# ---- terminal sidecar (pty-server.js) ----
# The node-pty WebSocket sidecar. Keep it on loopback — the browser never
# connects directly; server.js proxies /pty upgrades to PTY_HOST:PTY_PORT.
PTY_PORT=3001
PTY_HOST=127.0.0.1

# ---- public origin ----
# The origin users reach the app on (e.g. https://orch.example.com behind a
# Cloudflare Tunnel / reverse proxy). The client builds its ws(s):// terminal
# URL from it. Leave empty to use the browser's own origin (window.location),
# which is correct for any single-hostname deployment — including behind
# Cloudflare Access. Set it if your proxy REWRITES the Host header (Cloudflare
# Tunnel's httpHostHeader, say), since the origin gate then has no other way to
# learn the hostname the browser actually used.
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# Local mode accepts browser traffic from loopback origins by default
# (localhost, *.localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1). PUBLIC_BASE_URL is also trusted
# automatically. To intentionally open a local-mode instance on a LAN or
# another reverse-proxy origin, list exact comma-separated http(s) origins
# here, including non-default ports. Do not use this instead of authentication
# for an internet-exposed instance.
# Example: http://192.168.1.50:3000,https://operator.internal
ORCH_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Lets the pty sidecar accept connections from off-machine. It otherwise
# requires a loopback peer, since server.js proxies to it from the same host —
# a check the caller cannot forge, unlike a header. Only for a deliberately
# split app/sidecar deployment: anything that reaches the sidecar gets a shell.
ORCH_PTY_ALLOW_REMOTE=

# ---- web push (lib/push) ----
# Notifications pushed to subscribed devices (Settings → Notifications) are
# signed with a VAPID key. VAPID_SUBJECT is the contact the browsers' push
# services may use about the traffic: a mailto: or https: URL. Default:
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL when it is https, else mailto:admin@localhost. iOS/iPadOS
# push needs a REAL subject: Apple rejects mailto:admin@localhost with 403
# BadJwtToken, so set this (or PUBLIC_BASE_URL) to your https origin or a real
# mailto: for iPhone push. Chrome/Android/Firefox accept the default.
VAPID_SUBJECT=

# The VAPID private key, base64url raw P-256 scalar (the format
# `npx web-push generate-vapid-keys` prints). Leave empty and the instance
# mints one on first use at <ORCH_DB_DIR>/vapid.json. Every subscription is
# bound to the key it was made under, so keep the key with the database; set
# this only to hold one key across instances sharing a database or to restore
# subscriptions from a backup onto a fresh data dir.
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ---- Cloudflare Access (origin-side auth; optional) ----
# If you expose an instance to the internet behind Cloudflare Access, these make
# the ORIGIN verify the same identity (the Cf-Access-Jwt-Assertion JWT) on every
# route AND on the /pty terminal WebSocket, so reaching the port directly grants
# nothing. Enforcement is ON iff BOTH are set — leave empty for local use.
#   CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN: team domain, e.g. your-team.cloudflareaccess.com
#   CF_ACCESS_AUD:         the Access application's aud tag (comma-separable)
CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=
CF_ACCESS_AUD=

# Shared secret letting health probes (Docker HEALTHCHECK, uptime monitors) read
# the service-token routes — GET /api/version and /api/instance/usage, and ONLY
# those — without an Access JWT, via an `x-service-token: <value>` header.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# It also authenticates the two other callers that live INSIDE the box and have
# no Access JWT to present: the boot restore of managed services, and the stdio
# MCP bridge the non-Claude agents' tool calls go through. So with CF_ACCESS_*
# set this stops being optional in practice — the container image mints a
# per-boot token when you leave it empty (docker/entrypoint.sh), and a bare-node
# deployment warns at startup. Set it explicitly when something outside the box
# needs to poll.
SERVICE_TOKEN=

# Optional SECOND, fleet-wide READ token, accepted on the same two read-only
# routes (usage / version) and nowhere else — never on the mutating internal
# agent-tool endpoints, which demand the per-instance SERVICE_TOKEN.
# The point is a metrics dashboard that polls many boxes: it carries this one
# shared secret instead of learning each instance's private token. Unset (the
# default) = no such bypass exists. Only useful if you run a fleet.
ORCH_FLEET_TOKEN=

# ---- data locations ----
# SQLite app data (orchestrator.db) and per-task git worktrees. Both must live
# outside any project repo. Use absolute paths — `~` is NOT expanded.
# Defaults: $HOME/.zen-orchestrator and $HOME/.agent-orchestrator/worktrees.
ORCH_DB_DIR=
ORCH_WORKTREES_DIR=

# One app process per orchestrator.db. Two processes against one database
# corrupt each other: boot clears what a crash left behind (running flags,
# queued follow-ups, open permission cards, in-flight schedule runs), and run
# against a LIVE instance that pass wipes work in progress. So the app locks the
# database at boot and refuses to start if another process holds it, naming the
# holder. Set `off` to skip the check — unsupported, and exactly what it
# prevents. Two instances want two ORCH_DB_DIRs, not this.
# Default: on.
ORCH_DB_LOCK=
# How long boot retries the lock before giving up. The wait covers a predecessor
# still shutting down; a crashed one releases instantly. Default: 10000.
ORCH_DB_LOCK_WAIT_MS=

# Where "New project → Clone from GitHub" puts cloned repos. Absolute path.
# Default: $HOME/projects.
ORCH_PROJECTS_DIR=

# ---- git remotes ----
# The app fetches a project's base branch (best-effort) when the project is
# opened and again before cutting a new task worktree, so tasks start from the
# real tip rather than a local `main` that went stale the moment a PR merged on
# GitHub. It never pulls, never moves your local branch on its own, and never
# blocks a task launch — the worst case is a slower Start and the purely local
# behaviour. Set to off/0/false for an air-gapped instance or a repo whose
# fetch is too expensive to do routinely. Default: on.
ORCH_GIT_FETCH=

# Hard deadline for that fetch, in ms. The subprocess is killed at this point
# and the launch continues from the best local ref. Default: 10000.
ORCH_GIT_FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS=

# How long a successful fetch counts as fresh, in ms. Opening a project and
# immediately launching several tasks costs one fetch, not one each.
# Default: 15000.
ORCH_GIT_FETCH_COOLDOWN_MS=

# ---- managed services ----
# Base of the deterministic per-project port block. Each project is assigned
# base + slot at creation (stored on projects.port) and that PORT is injected
# into its supervised dev/setup/test services and its PTY shell. Relocate to
# avoid clashing with the app/sidecar ports. Default: 4300.
ORCH_SERVICE_PORT_BASE=

# Per-service in-memory log ring buffer, in lines. Bounds what the supervisor
# keeps of each service's stdout/stderr for the Services drawer. Default: 1500.
ORCH_SERVICE_LOG_LINES=

# Public service hostnames (<slug>--<appHost>, e.g. calc--orch.example.com):
# set to 1 to let server.js reverse-proxy service hostnames to the local ports,
# with per-service visibility (private / shared link / public). OFF by default
# and deliberately separate from ORCH_FEATURE_SERVICES — enabling managed
# services must never expose anything publicly on its own. Requires
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL (the instance's own hostname is derived from it) plus
# wildcard DNS/TLS covering *--<that host>; without all of that, services keep
# their honest http://localhost:<port> URLs.
ORCH_SERVICE_HOSTS=

# ---- claude binary ----
# Path to the logged-in `claude` CLI (Pro/Max). Pinned because Next's server may
# run with a trimmed PATH. Default: $HOME/.local/bin/claude.
CLAUDE_CLI_PATH=

# ---- codex binary ----
# Path to the `codex` CLI the Codex agent driver drives (ChatGPT-plan login).
# Empty = the SDK auto-resolves the binary bundled with @openai/codex, and the
# auth helpers fall back to `codex` on PATH (installed globally in the image).
# Set to pin a specific binary when PATH is trimmed. Default: (auto-resolve).
CODEX_CLI_PATH=

# ---- gh binary ----
# Path to the GitHub CLI (`gh`) used for repo listing/cloning, the guided
# device-flow login, and opening PRs. Empty = auto-resolve: bare `gh` if the
# server's PATH can see it, else a best-effort probe of the usual install dirs
# (/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/bin, /opt/homebrew/bin, /usr/local/bin,
# /snap/bin, ~/.local/bin). The server process never reads a shell profile, so
# a gh that works in your terminal (Homebrew, snap) can be invisible to the
# app's PATH — set this to its full path if the probe misses yours.
# Default: (auto-resolve).
ORCH_GH_BIN=

# ---- tool permissions (Claude, "Accept edits" / "Plan mode") ----
# How long a permission prompt parks waiting for a decision while at least one
# browser tab is watching, in milliseconds. Generous by default so nobody hits
# it mid-review, but bounded so a prompt left overnight releases the turn
# instead of holding the task "running" (and the instance busy) forever.
# 0 = park indefinitely, like an unanswered question card. Default: 14400000 (4h).
ORCH_PERMISSION_PROMPT_TIMEOUT_MS=

# The same deadline when NOBODY is watching — an auto-started task, or any turn
# running while the app is closed. Nobody can answer, so the prompt fails closed
# quickly rather than wedging the turn; the window exists so a tab opening
# moments later still gets to decide. 0 = always use the attended timeout above.
# Default: 45000 (45s).
ORCH_PERMISSION_UNATTENDED_MS=

# The `approval_policy` the Codex driver passes to the CLI. Default "never"
# (unattended auto-run in isolated worktrees). Enterprise-managed Codex
# deployments can disallow "never" — Calandria detects the CLI's downgrade
# warning and switches itself to "on-request" from the next turn on, so
# usually nothing needs to be set here. To pick a policy up front use
# "on-request" or "on-failure", or "inherit" to omit the override so
# ~/.codex/config.toml and the managed requirements decide. Do NOT use
# "untrusted"/UnlessTrusted: Calandria's non-interactive sessions can't answer
# its approval prompts, so every command is rejected (it maps to "on-request").
CODEX_APPROVAL_POLICY=

# Whether Codex tasks also mount the MCP servers from your ~/.codex/config.toml,
# alongside the orchestrator's own bridge. Default off, unlike Claude tasks
# (which do inherit ~/.claude MCP servers): `codex exec` has no approver, so an
# inherited server's tools are visible to the model but every call comes back as
# "user cancelled MCP tool call". The driver unmounts them rather than dangle
# tools that can't work. Set to 1 to mount them anyway — e.g. if you've set
# default_tools_approval_mode = "approve" on your own servers.
CODEX_INHERIT_MCP=

# ---- internal agent-tool bridge ----
# The loopback origin the stdio MCP bridge (scripts/orch-mcp.mjs, spawned by the
# Codex CLI per turn) uses to POST suggest_task / expose_service back to the app's
# internal endpoints. Authenticated with SERVICE_TOKEN. Empty = http://127.0.0.1
# on the app's own PORT, correct for the in-container default. Override only if
# the app is reached differently from inside the box.
ORCH_INTERNAL_BASE_URL=

# ---- agent credentials (usually NOT set here) ----
# The default, recommended path is subscription login done IN THE APP:
#   - Claude: onboarding / Settings "Connect Claude"   → login state in ~/.claude
#   - Codex:  Settings → Agents "Connect Codex" (device auth) → ~/.codex
# Both credential dirs live on the per-user home volume, so logins survive
# restarts with no env vars.
#
# The "I have an API key instead" paths persist their key to a 0600 file under
# ORCH_DB_DIR (anthropic-api-key / openai-api-key) and re-apply it to the process
# env on boot; you do NOT normally set these here.
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY / ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN / OPENAI_API_KEY found in the launch
# environment are STRIPPED at boot (lib/env-keys.mjs + docker/entrypoint.sh,
# with a WARN in the log). A key inherited from a shell profile, environment.d,
# or a systemd unit would otherwise silently bill every turn per-token while
# the UI reports your subscription login. To deliberately seed a key from the
# environment for a headless/dev instance, opt in:
#   ORCH_ALLOW_API_KEY_ENV=1   keep env-provided keys; turns bill them per-token
#                              (the UI will report API-key billing)
ORCH_ALLOW_API_KEY_ENV=

# ---- per-instance resource caps (docker-compose.yml) ----
# What docker compose passes to the container runtime for THIS instance
# (--cpus / --memory). Defaults: 2 CPUs, 4g memory.
ORCH_CPUS=
ORCH_MEM=

# ---- image (docker-compose.yml) ----
# Which image the compose service runs. Empty = calandria:latest, i.e. what
# `docker build -t calandria .` produces from this checkout. Point it at the
# published multi-arch build to skip building entirely (see docs/SELF_HOSTING.md):
#   ORCH_IMAGE=ghcr.io/calandria-dev/calandria:latest
ORCH_IMAGE=

# ---- feature flags ----
# Unset/empty keeps each flag's shipped default; an explicit value decides.
# In-progress flags default OFF; shipped flags default ON.

# PREVIEW tab (project live-URL view). Off until the remote-execution backend
# that actually runs/hosts the app and serves a real URL is in place. Set to 1
# to surface the tab in the session rail. Default: off.
ORCH_FEATURE_LIVE_PREVIEW=

# Command palette: the toolbar "Jump to project, session, or command…" search
# bar and its ⌘K / Ctrl-K shortcut. Fuzzy-jumps to any project or session and
# runs common commands. Set to 1 to enable. Default: off.
ORCH_FEATURE_OMNI_SEARCH=

# TEST-ONLY: registers the deterministic mock agent driver (lib/agents/mock/)
# in the agent registry. Set automatically by the Playwright e2e suite (see
# e2e/README.md) so turns run without a real agent CLI or login. Never set this
# on a real instance — the mock would appear in the agent picker. Default: off.
ORCH_E2E_MOCK_AGENT=

# Managed Services — the toolbar "Services" button/drawer, the Services command
# config (dev/setup/test) block in the project-context editor, and the persisted
# supervisor: registry rows survive restarts, managed dev servers auto-restart
# on boot (with orphaned process groups from a crashed server reaped first),
# and port conflicts surface as a readable error instead of a crash loop.
# Default: ON — set to 0 to disable. Public service hostnames are NOT included:
# they stay opt-in via ORCH_SERVICE_HOSTS above, so this flag alone exposes
# nothing publicly.
ORCH_FEATURE_SERVICES=

# ---- scheduled tasks (lib/scheduler.ts) ----
# Master switch for the schedule ticker. On by default; off/0/false stops this
# instance from ever starting scheduled work on its own. Default: on.
ORCH_SCHEDULER=

# How often the ticker wakes to check for due firings, in ms. Default: 30000.
ORCH_SCHEDULE_TICK_MS=

# How late a missed firing may still run, in ms. Past this it is recorded as
# `missed` instead — never skipped silently. 0 disables catch-up.
# Default: 14400000 (4h).
ORCH_SCHEDULE_CATCHUP_MS=

# How long the fire-time slash-command check may take before the sweep gives up
# on it, in ms. It spawns the agent CLI inside the ticker's single-flight sweep,
# so an unbounded one would wedge every schedule on the instance. Default: 20000.
ORCH_SCHEDULE_PROBE_MS=

# ---- subscription plan usage (lib/agents/claude/planUsage.ts) ----
# The titlebar session/week usage meter for a Claude Pro/Max login. Percentages
# come from the same OAuth usage endpoint the Claude CLI's /usage panel reads
# (cached instance-wide, fetched only while a tab is polling), overlaid with the
# free rate-limit telemetry every turn already carries. Off/0/false hides the
# meter and guarantees the app never calls the usage endpoint. Default: on.
ORCH_PLAN_USAGE=

# Floor between two fetches of the provider usage endpoint, in ms. Anthropic
# rate-limits it aggressively; 300000 (5 min) matches the Claude CLI's own
# minimum interval for the same endpoint. Default: 300000.
ORCH_PLAN_USAGE_MIN_FETCH_MS=
